<<<
not be constructed with a participle unless that participle would have been used in the unabridged clause; but that would not have been though I:am sympathizing, but though I sympathize; contrast with this
===
itself that suggests a restriction: *though*, & other conjunctions, must not be constructed with a participle unless that participle would have been used in the unabridged clause; but that would not have been *though I am sympathizing*, but *though I sympathize*; contrast with this
>>>

<<<
 the perfectly satisfactory Though living he is no longer conscious, where the full form would be not Though he lives, but Though he is living.
===
 the perfectly satisfactory *Though living he is no longer conscious*, where the full form would be not *Though he lives*, but *Though he is living.*
>>>

<<<
4. Illogical use. The danger of using adversative conjunctions where two propositions are not strictly opposed, but in harmony, is explained & illustrated in BUT, 3.
===
**4.** Illogical use. The danger of using adversative conjunctions where two propositions are not strictly opposed, but in harmony, is explained & illustrated in ^^BUT^^, 3.
>>>

<<<
In the following example, though would be right if the words ‘is the only country in Europe that °’ were not there; as it stands, the sentence is nonsense:
===
In the following example, *though* would be right if the words ' is the only country in Europe that ' were not there; as it stands, the sentence is nonsense:
>>>

<<<
Though it is only in recent times that in England the Jewish civil disabilities were repeated, Turkey is the only country in Murope that has throughout been free of any anti-Jewish propaganda.
===
*Though it is only in recent times that in England the Jewish civil disabilities were repeated, Turkey is the only country in Europe that has throughout been free of any anti-Jewish propaganda.*
>>>

<<<
thral(l)dom. See -LL-, -L-, 4.
===
**thral(l)dom.** See ^^-LL-, -L-^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
thrash, thresh. One word, with two pronunciations & spellings differentiated. To separate grain is almost always -esh; to flog is always -ash;
===
**thrash, thresh.** One word, with two pronunciations & spellings differentiated. To separate grain is almost always *-esh*; to flog is always *-ash*;
>>>

<<<
thrasonical. See PEDANTIC HUMOUR.
===
**thrasonical.** See ^^PEDANTIC HUMOUR^^.
>>>

<<<
Threadneedle Street, Old Lady of. See SOBRIQUETS.
===
**Threadneedle Street, Old Lady of.** See ^^SOBRIQUETS^^.
>>>

<<<
threaten. The Mass Vestments, now threatened to be authoritatively revived, have to be decided upon. See DOUBLE PASSIVE.
===
**threaten.** *The Mass Vestments, now threatened to be authoritatively revived, have to be decided upon.* See ^^DOUBLE PASSIVE^^.
>>>

<<<
three-quarter(s). The noun expressing a fraction has the -s, &, though usually hyphened, is better written as two separate words; see Hypruens, 3B.
===
**three-quarter(s).** The noun expressing a fraction has the -s, &, though usually hyphened, is better written as two separate words; see ^^HYPHENS^^, 3 B.
>>>

<<<
This noun is often used attributively with another noun, e.g. with back at football, or with length or face in portraiture;
===
This noun is often used attributively with another noun, e.g. with *back* at football, or with *length* or *face* in portraiture;
>>>

<<<
+ purpose, even if that singular does not otherwise exist (shear steel, not shears steel; scissor-shaped, not scissors-shaped; racket-court, not rackets-court;_ cf., however, fivescourt, breeches-maker, &c.);
===
+ purpose, even if that singular does not otherwise exist (*shear steel*, not *shears steel*; *scissor-shaped*, not *scissors-shaped*; *racket-court*, not *rackets-court*; cf., however, *fives-court, breeches-maker*, &c.);
>>>

<<<
accordingly, three-quarter back & threequarter face are the normal forms. But the nouns back, length, &c., are often dropped when context allows,
===
accordingly, *three-quarter back* & *three-quarter face* are the normal forms. But the nouns *back, length*, &c., are often dropped when context allows,
>>>

<<<
being then an elliptical noun, it has a tendency to resume its -s, & a three-quarters is perhaps more often seen than a three-quarter; either is legitimate.
===
being then an elliptical noun, it has a tendency to resume its -s, & *a three-quarters* is perhaps more often seen than *a three-quarter*; either is legitimate.
>>>

<<<
threnody, threnode. The OED treats the first as the standard form.
===
**threnody, threnode.** The OED treats the first as the standard form.
>>>

<<<
thrive. The OED gives throve, thriven, as the past & p.p., but allows thrived for either.
===
**thrive.** The OED gives *throve, thriven*, as the past & p.p., but allows *thrived* for either.
>>>

<<<
throat. For a lump in one’s t., see Srock PATHOS.
===
**throat.** For *a lump in* one's *t.*, see ^^STOCK PATHOS^^.
>>>

<<<
throstle. The -t- is silent; see PRONUNCIATION.
===
**throstle.** The *-t-* is silent; see ^^PRONUNCIATION^^.
>>>

<<<
thunderer. For the T.=The Times, see SOBRIQUETS.
===
**thunderer.** For *the T.*=The Times, see ^^SOBRIQUETS^^.
>>>

<<<
Thursday. For the adverbial use, see FRIDAY.
===
**Thursday.** For the adverbial use, see ^^FRIDAY^^.
>>>

<<<
thus. There is a particular use of thus that should be carefully avoided; it is very common in the newspapers, & the fact that the OED nevertheless does not quote a single example of it probably indicates that it is very uncommon elsewhere.
===
**thus.** There is a particular use of *thus* that should be carefully avoided; it is very common in the newspapers, & the fact that the OED nevertheless does not quote a single example of it probably indicates that it is very uncommon elsewhere.
>>>

<<<
In this use thus is placed before a present participle (thus enabling &c.), & its function,
===
In this use *thus* is placed before a present participle (*thus enabling* &c.), & its function,
>>>

<<<
or whether there is any noun to which it can properly be attached (cf. UNAT- TACHED PARTICIPLES); the exact content of thus itself is often as difficult to ascertain as the allegiance of the participle.
===
or whether there is any noun to which it can properly be attached (cf. ^^UNATTACHED PARTICIPLES^^); the exact content of *thus* itself is often as difficult to ascertain as the allegiance of the participle.
>>>
